74. Grammar Must Not Execute Code

Parsing and grammar validation must not:

- execute user programs;
- contact hardware;
- contact networks;
- access arbitrary files;
- submit QPU jobs;
- query calibration;
- modify machine state;
- invoke arbitrary foreign code.

The grammar frontend is a pure source-processing boundary.

---

75. Security

Grammar validation must treat source code as untrusted input.

Required protections include:

- bounded parser recursion where technically required;
- controlled macro expansion;
- diagnostic count limits;
- source-size limits as infrastructure controls;
- no arbitrary filesystem access;
- no network access;
- no command execution;
- no unsafe Rust;
- deterministic processing.

Security controls must not alter language semantics.

---

76. Reproducibility

Parsing the same source with the same grammar version must produce identical results.

Build metadata must not influence syntax.

Environment variables must not silently change grammar semantics.

Hardware must not influence parsing.

---

77. Environment Independence

The parser must not behave differently because:

- GPU count changed;
- QPU changed;
- CPU architecture changed;
- memory size changed;
- machine topology changed;
- runtime backend changed.

Target selection occurs after semantic parsing.

---

78. Test Classification

Every grammar feature must have at least:

Positive

Valid syntax.

Negative

Invalid syntax.

Boundary

Minimum and large symbolic structures.

Regression

Previously discovered bugs.

Compatibility

Version behavior.

Determinism

Repeated parsing.

Round-trip

Parse/print/parse where supported.

Integration

Interaction with downstream semantic layers.

---

79. Golden Tests

Stable source examples should have expected:

- token streams where appropriate;
- parse trees where appropriate;
- diagnostics;
- AST serialization;
- semantic representation.

Golden outputs must be versioned.

Changes must be reviewed rather than silently regenerated.

---

80. Fuzz Testing

The grammar frontend should be fuzz-tested for:

- crashes;
- hangs;
- excessive memory growth;
- infinite recovery;
- non-determinism;
- malformed Unicode;
- deeply nested expressions;
- malformed comments;
- malformed strings;
- malformed numeric literals;
- malformed quantum syntax;
- malformed HDL syntax.

Fuzzing must not require unsafe Rust in Zamani.

---

81. Property Testing

Properties should include:

parse(source) is deterministic
valid(source) => parse succeeds
invalid(source) => diagnostic exists
parse(print(parse(source))) preserves structure

where the printer is defined for that representation.

---

82. Round-Trip Validation

For supported syntax:

source
  -> parse
  -> AST
  -> canonical printer
  -> parse

must preserve semantic structure.

Whitespace and formatting need not be preserved.

Meaning must be preserved.

---

83. Canonical Formatting

Formatting must not change semantics.

Formatting must not:

- select a hardware target;
- change qubit allocation;
- change scheduling;
- alter resource requirements;
- alter capability requirements.

---

84. Versioned Grammar Artifacts

Generated parser artifacts must never be treated as hand-maintained source.

The source of truth remains the grammar specification.

Generated artifacts must contain provenance identifying:

- grammar version;
- generator version;
- generation mode;
- source grammar;
- generation timestamp if useful for diagnostics, but not for semantic determinism.

---

85. ANTLR Integration

The ANTLR grammar must be validated in CI.

Required checks:

ANTLR grammar generation succeeds
lexer generation succeeds
parser generation succeeds
generated sources compile
parser tests pass
negative tests pass

Grammar generation failures are production failures.

---

86. Generated Code Boundary

Generated ANTLR code must not be manually patched.

If generated code is wrong:

fix grammar

rather than:

patch generated parser

Generated files should be reproducible.

---

87. Rust Integration

The Rust frontend should expose a stable abstraction similar in responsibility to:

source
  -> lexer
  -> parser
  -> diagnostics
  -> AST
  -> semantic validation

The exact repository module names may differ.

The grammar validation document does not require a particular Rust AST implementation.

---

88. No Unsafe Rust

The entire grammar frontend integration must compile with:

#![deny(unsafe_code)]
#![deny(unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn)]

CI must verify this.

---

89. Error API

Grammar errors should have a stable category boundary.

Conceptually:

LexError
ParseError
GrammarVersionError
RecoveryError
DiagnosticError

These must not be confused with:

TypeError
CapabilityError
ResourceError
TargetError
RuntimeError

---

90. Error Conversion

Conversions between error layers must preserve the original cause.

For example:

lexer error
    ->
frontend error

must not become a generic:

compiler failed

without preserving source context.

---

91. No Backend Leakage

Grammar validation must not import:

QPU backend
GPU backend
FPGA backend
CPU backend
cloud backend

merely to parse source code.

Backend dependencies belong downstream.

---

92. No Scheduler Leakage

Grammar validation must not depend on scheduler implementation details.

The grammar may parse:

schedule
timing
ordering
latency

but must not depend on scheduler algorithms.

---

93. No Optimization Leakage

Grammar validation must not invoke:

- peephole optimization;
- cancellation;
- T-gate reduction;
- routing optimization;
- circuit optimization.

Those happen after semantic lowering.

---

94. No QEC Leakage

Grammar validation must not instantiate:

- decoders;
- syndrome graphs;
- QEC schedules;
- stabilizer algorithms.

The grammar only establishes syntax and intent.

---

95. No ZQN Leakage

Grammar validation must not instantiate noise channels.

ZQN semantics are downstream.

---

96. Capability Resolution

Capability expressions must be represented independently of the current machine.

For example:

requires quantum;
requires capability("entanglement");

must remain valid even when the current compilation target lacks the capability.

The target resolver later reports feasibility.

---

97. Portability Rule

A source program should remain semantically portable unless it explicitly opts into target-specific semantics.

Portability must be the default.

---

98. Target-Specific Escape Hatch

Target-specific constructs are allowed where necessary, but they must be explicit.

Conceptually:

target-specific {
    ...
}

or an equivalent namespaced mechanism.

Such code must be clearly marked as non-portable.

---

99. Portability Diagnostics

When target-specific constructs occur in a supposedly portable compilation unit, diagnostics should identify:

- construct;
- target dependency;
- portability consequence;
- possible portable alternative if known.

---

100. Resource Availability

The language must distinguish:

program requires resource

from:

resource is currently available

This is fundamental to POCO-REAF.

---

101. POCO-REAF Validation

The grammar passes the POCO-REAF validation requirement if:

1. source semantics do not require a fixed machine;
2. resource quantities can remain symbolic;
3. target details are separated;
4. canonical IR preserves semantics;
5. downstream compilation can adapt implementation;
6. hardware feasibility is checked after parsing;
7. syntax does not impose artificial physical limits.

---

102. Compile-Once Semantics

"Compile once" must not mean that one physical binary can execute unchanged on every architecture.

It means the semantic compilation artifact must preserve enough target-independent information for downstream realization.

The grammar must therefore preserve:

- semantic intent;
- requirements;
- constraints;
- capabilities;
- provenance;
- version information.

---

103. Run-Everywhere Semantics

The validator must never reject a program solely because:

current_target != future_target

unless the source explicitly requires that target.

---

104. Future Hardware

The grammar must be extensible to hardware that does not yet exist.

Do not make the core grammar exhaustive over all possible future machines.

Use extensible:

capabilities
dialects
resource expressions
target descriptions
operations
interfaces

instead.

---

105. Future Computing Models

The same principle applies to future computation paradigms.

A future domain should be integrable without rewriting unrelated:

- lexical rules;
- expressions;
- statements;
- types;
- modules;
- classical constructs.

---

106. Extension Contract

Every new grammar domain must specify:

syntax owner
semantic owner
canonical IR
resource model
capability model
target integration
diagnostics
compatibility
tests

No domain may be added without these contracts.

---

107. Domain Isolation

The following domains must remain independently testable:

classical
quantum
hybrid
HDL
hardware
distributed
AI
data
networking
security

Cross-domain syntax may compose them without forcing their implementations to depend cyclically on each other.

---

108. Dependency Direction

The intended dependency direction is:

specification
    |
    v
lexer
    |
    v
core syntax
    |
    v
types
    |
    v
expressions
    |
    v
statements
    |
    v
domain syntax
    |
    v
AST
    |
    v
semantic validation
    |
    v
canonical IR
    |
    v
compiler

The reverse direction is forbidden.

---

109. Circular Dependency Rule

Forbidden:

grammar -> runtime -> grammar
grammar -> hardware -> grammar
grammar -> quantum::ir -> grammar

Allowed:

grammar -> semantic lowering -> quantum::ir

and:

grammar -> resource intent -> resource validation

---

110. File Completion Contract

Each validation-related file must be independently completable.

Before implementation, every file must define:

Purpose
Owns
Does Not Own
Inputs
Outputs
Dependencies
Upstream Contracts
Downstream Consumers
AST Contract
Semantic Contract
IR Integration
Compiler Integration
Runtime Integration
Tests
Negative Tests
Boundary Tests
Compatibility
Scalability
Hard-Coding Audit
Completion Criteria

No file may rely on an unspecified future redesign.

---

111. Validation Files

The validation directory should contain, where justified:

grammar-validation.md
semantic-boundaries.md
ambiguity-rules.md
scalability-rules.md
compatibility-rules.md
naming-rules.md
hardcoding-audit.md

"grammar-validation.md" is the primary validation contract.

The other documents refine specialized areas and must not contradict this document.

---

112. Hard-Coding Audit

Every grammar change must be checked for:

fixed counts
fixed dimensions
fixed device IDs
fixed topology
fixed qubit IDs
fixed core counts
fixed GPU counts
fixed node counts
fixed memory
fixed register sizes
fixed timing
fixed architecture

Every occurrence must be classified.

---

113. Hard-Coding Classification

Each discovered constant must be classified as:

A. Language semantic constant

Required by the language itself.

B. Lexical constant

Required for parsing.

C. Security limit

Protects compiler infrastructure.

D. Representation limit

Required by a particular serialized representation.

E. Target constraint

Belongs to hardware/target validation.

F. Test fixture

Only exists in tests.

G. Accidental hard-code

Must be removed.

---

114. Fixed Mathematical Examples

A rule such as:

matrixDecl
    : ... INTEGER INTEGER ...

is not automatically a scalability violation.

The question is whether those integers are:

source-provided dimensions

or:

grammar-imposed dimensions

Only the latter is prohibited.

---

115. Fixed Quantum Examples

A rule that accepts:

q[0]

is syntactically valid.

It becomes a scalability violation only if the language itself assumes:

there are exactly q[0] ... q[N]

with an arbitrary fixed "N".

Indices are program data.

Hardware mapping is downstream.

---

116. Fixed Gate Examples

Built-in gates may be defined as standard vocabulary.

But the grammar must permit extension through:

- declarations;
- namespaces;
- libraries;
- dialects;
- operation identifiers.

The language must not become permanently limited to today's gate vocabulary.

---

117. Validation of Annotations

Annotations must be validated according to ownership.

Core annotations belong to core language semantics.

Domain annotations belong to their domain.

Unknown annotations may be permitted only under an explicit extension policy.

An annotation must not silently change semantics without a declared semantic owner.

---

118. Metadata

Metadata must remain distinct from executable semantics unless explicitly declared semantic.

Compiler metadata may include:

source provenance
documentation
optimization hints
diagnostic information
tooling information

Metadata must not silently become hardware requirements.

---

119. Comments

Comments are non-semantic unless explicitly designated as structured documentation or directives.

A comment must never be used as a substitute for an actual AST/IR operation.

In particular, a backend must never emit an unimplemented operation as a comment and claim semantic correctness.

---

120. Missing Feature Rule

A syntax feature is not production-ready merely because ANTLR accepts it.

It is production-ready only when:

grammar
+
AST
+
semantic validation
+
IR/lowering
+
diagnostics
+
tests
+
documentation

are complete for that feature.

---

121. Unsupported Feature Rule

If syntax is recognized but semantics are not implemented, the compiler must produce a clear unsupported-feature diagnostic.

It must not silently:

- ignore the construct;
- turn it into a comment;
- delete it;
- approximate it without permission;
- change its meaning.

---

122. Semantic Preservation

Every lowering must preserve observable program semantics.

For quantum programs, this includes where applicable:

- operation ordering;
- qubit identity at the logical level;
- measurement semantics;
- classical control;
- reset semantics;
- parameter values;
- resource requirements;
- declared constraints.

---

123. Classical/Quantum Boundary

Classical values and quantum values must have explicit semantic boundaries.

A grammar must not allow ambiguous constructs where the compiler cannot determine whether an identifier refers to:

classical data
quantum resource
hardware resource
runtime resource

without semantic context.

---

124. Hybrid Programs

Hybrid programs must preserve:

quantum -> classical
classical -> quantum
measurement -> control
classical parameter -> quantum operation

relationships explicitly.

The grammar must not require a particular execution architecture.

---

125. Dynamic Quantum Programs

Dynamic circuits must be represented without assuming a fixed runtime implementation.

The language may express:

measure
if measured condition
apply operation
repeat
while condition

while the backend decides whether and how those operations can execute.

---

126. Timing

Timing syntax must distinguish:

timing intent

from:

physical calibrated duration

The grammar must not assume universal gate durations.

This is particularly important for quantum and HDL programs.

---

127. Duration Literals

Duration literals are semantic values.

Examples:

1 ns
10 us
2 ms

must not imply that every backend supports those exact timings.

Target validation determines feasibility.

---

128. Scheduling Constraints

A scheduling constraint may express:

before
after
within
deadline
latency
alignment
synchronization

but must not force a scheduler algorithm.

---

129. Memory Semantics

Memory syntax must distinguish:

logical memory
physical memory
allocation policy
placement

The language may express ownership and lifetime.

Physical allocation belongs downstream.

---

130. Concurrency

Concurrency syntax must not assume:

N threads
N cores

The number of execution resources is a target/runtime property.

The program describes concurrency semantics.

---

131. Parallelism

Parallel syntax must express:

parallel intent
data parallelism
task parallelism
independent work
reduction

without imposing physical worker counts.

---

132. Distributed Execution

Distributed syntax must remain independent of the number of nodes.

A program may describe:

replicate
partition
shard
communicate
place
migrate

without hard-coding a cluster size.

---

133. Networking

Network syntax must distinguish:

protocol
endpoint
message
transport
capability
security requirement

from a concrete machine address.

Addresses may be valid application data but must not become universal hardware assumptions.

---

134. Security Validation

Security syntax must be validated without executing cryptographic operations during parsing.

The grammar may parse:

capability
permission
identity
trust
cryptographic requirement

but actual cryptographic implementation belongs to the security subsystem.

---

135. Deterministic Hashing and Maps

Where validation needs maps or sets whose iteration affects:

- diagnostics;
- AST serialization;
- test output;

deterministic ordering must be used.

Do not expose randomized map iteration in compiler outputs.

---

136. Unicode

Unicode handling must be deterministic.

The grammar must define:

- valid identifier characters;
- normalization policy;
- invalid sequences;
- source encoding assumptions.

Normalization must not change identifier meaning unexpectedly.

---

137. Comments and Documentation

Comment handling must not interfere with tokenization.

Nested block comments must be explicitly specified if supported.

Documentation comments must be distinguishable from ordinary comments if tooling consumes them.

---

138. Whitespace

Whitespace must be insignificant unless explicitly defined as syntax.

If indentation becomes significant in a future dialect, it must be an explicit dialect feature rather than an accidental parser behavior.

---

139. Semicolon Policy

If semicolons are optional in some contexts, insertion rules must be deterministic.

The grammar must never rely on host-language parser assumptions.

---

140. Empty Programs

The grammar must define whether:

EOF

is a valid program.

If an empty source is valid, that must be intentional and tested.

---

141. Maximum Nesting

Parser nesting limits may exist for security.

They must be:

- implementation-level;
- configurable;
- documented;
- independent from source semantics.

A valid program must not become semantically invalid merely because one compiler invocation has an insufficient parser budget.

---

142. Large Source Files

Large source programs must be supported subject only to infrastructure resources.

Tests should use generated large inputs to detect:

- quadratic parsing;
- exponential ambiguity;
- memory leaks;
- catastrophic recovery.

---

143. Complexity

Grammar rules should avoid unnecessary exponential ambiguity.

Particular attention must be given to:

- expression parsing;
- nested patterns;
- generic/type parsing;
- macro syntax;
- dialect syntax;
- annotations;
- interpolation.

---

144. Ambiguity Policy

An ambiguity must be resolved through:

1. grammar restructuring;
2. explicit precedence;
3. lexical distinction;
4. contextual parsing only where justified.

Do not rely on undocumented parser-generator precedence behavior.

---

145. Left Recursion

Left recursion may be used only where ANTLR's supported mechanisms make the resulting grammar deterministic and maintainable.

Expression precedence should be explicit and tested.

---

146. Parser Performance

Production validation must benchmark:

- small programs;
- medium programs;
- very large programs;
- deeply nested programs;
- large quantum programs;
- large HDL programs;
- cross-domain programs.

Performance tests must detect asymptotic regressions.

---

147. No Host-Architecture Assumptions

Grammar validation must not assume:

usize == 64 bits
pointer == 64 bits
little endian
specific CPU
specific OS
specific filesystem

Host representation belongs to the compiler/runtime.

---

148. Integer Index Validation

A source index is not automatically a machine address.

Semantic validation determines whether an index is valid for a particular object.

Hardware addressing is separate.

---

149. Memory Address Syntax

Raw addresses, if supported, must be explicitly unsafe/low-level language semantics.

However, the repository requirement is:

Zamani Rust implementation uses no unsafe Rust

This does not prohibit a Zamani language feature named "unsafe"; it means the compiler implementation itself must remain safe Rust.

The distinction must be documented.

---

150. Existing "unsafe" Grammar

The current grammar includes an "unsafe" modifier/block.

This must be reviewed as a Zamani language construct, not confused with Rust's implementation safety.

If retained, its semantic contract must be explicit.

The Rust compiler implementation must still use no Rust "unsafe".

---

151. Mathematical Operations

Math syntax must not hard-code algorithm implementations.

For example:

fft
svd
qr

may be language-level operations.

Their implementation may vary by target.

The semantic meaning must remain stable.

---

152. Optimization Algorithms

Named optimization algorithms should not imply that the compiler must implement one specific numerical algorithm in the grammar layer.

Prefer semantic operations plus explicit algorithm selection when algorithm choice itself is meaningful.

---

153. Numerical Tolerances

Numerical tolerances must be values expressed by the program or policy.

The grammar must not embed arbitrary defaults such as:

tolerance = 0.000001

as universal semantics.

---

154. AI/ML Validation

AI syntax must remain portable.

Model dimensions, tensor shapes, device placement, accelerator choice, and batch sizes should be parameterizable.

GPU/TPU/NPU availability belongs to capability resolution.

---

155. Data Validation

Data syntax must distinguish:

logical schema
physical storage
deployment
transport

A schema must not implicitly require a particular database or filesystem.

---

156. Deployment

Deployment syntax may describe intent.

The compiler/runtime decides actual placement.

Deployment syntax must not leak into ordinary computation semantics unless deployment is itself part of the program.

---

157. Plugin Validation

Plugins must have explicit boundaries.

A plugin must not silently modify the core grammar.

Grammar extension must go through declared dialect/extension mechanisms.

---

158. Grammar Extension

Any grammar extension must specify:

namespace
version
lexer impact
parser impact
AST impact
semantic owner
IR impact
diagnostics
tests
compatibility

Extensions that modify core lexical meaning require especially strict review.

---

159. Reserved Syntax

Unused syntax may be reserved explicitly.

Reserved syntax must not accidentally parse as a different valid construct.

---

160. Deprecation

Deprecated syntax must produce a deterministic warning where appropriate.

Deprecation must specify:

- since version;
- replacement;
- removal policy;
- migration path.

---

161. Compatibility Matrix

Every grammar release must be testable against:

previous valid programs
current valid programs
known-invalid programs
deprecated programs
reserved syntax

---

162. Source Compatibility

A new compiler should preserve semantics of supported source programs.

If syntax changes but semantics remain equivalent, a migration tool may transform old source.

---

163. Binary/IR Compatibility

Grammar validation itself does not own binary compatibility.

However, the source version must be retained sufficiently for downstream IR/artifact compatibility.

---

164. Provenance

The frontend should preserve:

source language version
grammar version
dialect versions
source origin

where required by the compiler architecture.

---

165. Reproducible Compilation

Grammar validation must not depend on:

- current time;
- random values;
- machine ID;
- network;
- backend state.

If source code contains nondeterministic semantics, that nondeterminism must be explicit.

---

166. Test Corpus

The grammar test corpus must contain:

minimal program
classical program
generic program
concurrent program
distributed program
quantum program
hybrid program
HDL program
hardware program
AI program
accelerator program
full-stack program
POCO-REAF program

---

167. Regression Corpus

Every grammar bug becomes a permanent regression test.

The regression test must document:

bug
root cause
correct behavior
incorrect behavior

---

168. Negative Corpus

Negative tests must cover:

- duplicate operators;
- malformed literals;
- invalid declarations;
- missing delimiters;
- invalid nesting;
- ambiguous syntax;
- malformed generics;
- malformed quantum operations;
- malformed HDL;
- malformed resource expressions;
- invalid annotations;
- invalid dialect declarations.

---

169. Cross-Domain Negative Tests

Examples:

classical operation on quantum value
quantum operation on classical-only resource
hardware-only construct in portable context
invalid resource requirement
unknown capability
invalid dialect version

These should be distinguished between syntax and semantic errors.

---

170. Semantic Error Boundary

A syntactically valid but semantically invalid program must parse successfully.

For example:

use_unknown_type;

may be syntactically valid but semantically invalid.

The parser must not pretend semantic errors are syntax errors.

---

171. Target Error Boundary

A program requiring more resources than a selected target provides may still be a valid Zamani program.

Example:

program requirement = 1000 logical qubits
target capacity = 128 physical qubits

This is a target feasibility problem.

It is not necessarily a grammar error.

---

172. Capability Error Boundary

Similarly:

requires capability X
target lacks X

is a capability/target error, not a grammar error.

---

173. Scheduling Error Boundary

A valid timing requirement that cannot be satisfied by a target is a scheduling/target feasibility issue.

It is not a syntax error.

---

174. Optimization Failure Boundary

Failure to find a desired optimization is not a grammar error.

The original semantic program must remain available.

---

175. QEC Failure Boundary

Inability to satisfy a requested QEC strategy is not a syntax failure.

It is a QEC/capability/resource issue.

---

176. ZQN Failure Boundary

An unavailable noise model or unsupported fault channel is not automatically a grammar failure.

The source must be parsed first.

---

177. Validation Pipeline

The complete frontend validation pipeline should be:

source bytes
    |
    v
encoding validation
    |
    v
lexer
    |
    v
tokens
    |
    v
parser
    |
    v
parse tree
    |
    v
AST construction
    |
    v
AST structural validation
    |
    v
name validation
    |
    v
type validation
    |
    v
effect validation
    |
    v
capability/resource validation
    |
    v
domain semantic validation
    |
    v
canonical IR lowering

The exact Rust module boundaries may vary, but these responsibilities must remain distinguishable.

---

178. Validation Does Not Mean Execution

No validation stage should execute the user's actual computation.

Compile-time evaluation is permitted only where the language explicitly defines compile-time semantics.

---

179. Compile-Time Evaluation

Compile-time evaluation must have:

- deterministic semantics;
- resource accounting;
- explicit failure;
- no arbitrary external effects unless explicitly authorized.

---

180. Source Security

The frontend must treat all input as hostile.

It must not trust:

- identifiers;
- strings;
- annotations;
- dialect metadata;
- macro input;
- foreign-language payloads.

---

181. Foreign Source

Foreign source embedded in Zamani must have explicit boundaries.

For example:

foreign "C" { ... }

must not be interpreted as Zamani grammar.

---

182. Hardware Descriptions

HDL source must have an explicit syntactic domain.

A hardware module must not accidentally parse as an ordinary software module with different meaning.

---

183. Quantum/HDL Integration

Quantum control hardware may combine:

quantum semantics
+
hardware semantics

but ownership must remain explicit.

Quantum semantic meaning belongs to quantum IR.

Hardware implementation belongs to hardware/HDL layers.

---

184. No Semantic Duplication

If two grammar constructs mean the same thing, they should not create two incompatible AST concepts.

Aliases should converge to the same semantic representation.

---

185. Naming Consistency

Names must use one canonical naming policy.

Do not create accidental alternatives such as:

quantumCircuit
quantum_circuit
QuantumCircuit
qCircuit

unless they intentionally represent different language concepts.

---

186. Reserved Identifiers

Core keywords must be reserved consistently.

Domain-specific identifiers should preferably use namespaces rather than continuously expanding the global keyword set.

This reduces lexical collisions and improves long-term extensibility.

---

187. Namespace Design

Namespaces must support future domain expansion.

A quantum dialect must not need to reserve hundreds of globally unique keywords.

---

188. Extensibility Principle

Prefer:

namespace::operation

over continuously adding:

GLOBAL_KEYWORD_1
GLOBAL_KEYWORD_2
GLOBAL_KEYWORD_3

when the construct is domain-extensible.

---

189. Grammar Size

Grammar size itself is not a production-quality metric.

A smaller grammar is not automatically better.

The priorities are:

1. unambiguous semantics;
2. maintainability;
3. extensibility;
4. deterministic parsing;
5. clear ownership;
6. repository integration.

---

190. Generated Grammar Organization

If "Zamani.g4" becomes too large, it may be split into lexer/parser grammar components.

Any split must preserve:

one canonical grammar authority

and deterministic generation.

ANTLR imports must be explicit.

---

191. Modular Grammar Rule

If grammar modules are introduced, each module must document:

purpose
tokens
productions
dependencies
exports
semantic owner
tests

---

192. No Empty Grammar Files

A grammar file must not exist merely because a future feature might use it.

Create a file only when it has an implemented, testable responsibility.

---

193. Validation Document Completion

"grammar/validation/grammar-validation.md" is complete when:

- every validation boundary is defined;
- no machine-size assumptions remain;
- syntax/semantic/resource/target errors are separated;
- quantum integration is defined;
- IR ownership is defined;
- no unsafe Rust requirement exists;
- Rust 1.97/1.97.1 compatibility is defined;
- diagnostics are deterministic;
- testing requirements are defined;
- extension rules are defined;
- compatibility rules are defined.

