216. Final Validation Principle

The grammar must validate:

"What does this program mean?"

not:

"Can this particular machine execute it right now?"

Those are different questions.

The final architecture must therefore preserve:

SOURCE SEMANTICS
       |
       v
PORTABLE MEANING
       |
       v
RESOURCE REQUIREMENTS
       |
       v
TARGET CAPABILITIES
       |
       v
IMPLEMENTATION
       |
       v
EXECUTION

and never reverse that dependency.

---

217. POCO-REAF Acceptance Criterion

A grammar implementation satisfies POCO-REAF when the following statement is true:

«A valid Zamani source program expresses a stable computation whose semantic meaning does not depend on the accidental size, topology, vendor, architecture, calibration, device identifier, or current availability of the machine on which it will eventually execute.»

The compiler may choose different implementations.

The scheduler may choose different schedules.

The router may choose different physical mappings.

The optimizer may choose different implementations.

The hardware layer may select different resources.

The runtime may execute in different environments.

The program's semantic meaning remains the same.

That is the required foundation for:

Program_Once
Compile_Once
Run_Everywhere
Run_Anywhere
Run_Forever

and therefore for:

Zamani
From Atom to Everywhere